#{[17592186419785 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(2\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot 0)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-3)\\hspace{0.167em}((-2)\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot 2)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}0\\hspace{0.167em}((-2)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(-8)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-3)\\hspace{0.167em}(8)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}0\\hspace{0.167em}(-4) \\\\  & =-8+24+0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186419786 "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\mathbf{a}\\cdot ((6\\mathbf{b})\\times (4\\mathbf{c})) & =120 \\\\ (-5)\\cdot 6\\cdot 4\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =120 \\\\ (-120)[\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =120 \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186421760 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $5$ and $2,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={e}^{y}={e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={e}^{x}={e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitutions $u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,du=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt$ and $v=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,dv=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}t})\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}t)-({e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}t})\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}t})\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}t})\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}{e}^{u}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u+{\\int }_{1}^{1}{e}^{v}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186421761 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(e,1).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{n}$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf{T},$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/ba/ba6285008fd90835fdbf73203b76a0a01d713889d9939979aaec54bebd0a8c49.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}={t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x}={e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2})\\cdot (1)-({e}^{-t})\\cdot ({e}^{t})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1)-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186421762 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={e}^{y}={e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={e}^{x}={e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitutions $u=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,du=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt$ and $v=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,dv=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}t})\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}t)-({e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}t})\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}{e}^{u}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}u-{\\int }_{1}^{1}{e}^{v}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186421763 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x+y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}t)-(3\\operatorname{cos}t+5\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+9\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+15{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =15(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =30\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186421764 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{t},\\,t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{n}$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf{T},$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/cc/cc9d66f800a57f27673577cd3fd179b4cd6ff64585c5dc1a867317d8d592e485.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y=t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-y=-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(t)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\cdot (1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}0\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186423120 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-u-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,-2\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=-u-v} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}-(-u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(-u-v)^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}-(-u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(-u-v)^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot (1)+({v}^{2}-(-u-v)^{2})\\cdot (1)+(-u-v)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}+{v}^{2}-(-u-v)^{2}+(-u-v)^{2} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\color{blue}{u}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186423121 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z={x}^{2}y,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}y⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2xy⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2xy \\\\ 0 & 1 & {x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-2xy,\\hspace{0.222em}-{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨-2xy,\\hspace{0.222em}-{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({\\color{blue}-2P}\\hspace{0.167em}xy+(\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}-Q}\\hspace{0.167em})\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186423122 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{{\\color{blue}∂x}}-Q\\frac{{\\color{blue}∂g}}{∂y}+{\\color{blue}R})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186423123 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,-3\\le v\\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=v-u} \\\\  & =⟨(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 1+(u-v)^{2}\\cdot (-1)+uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5uv)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{\\color{blue}5uv}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186423124 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-3v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}2z,\\hspace{0.222em}y+z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=u-3v} \\\\  & =⟨u+u-3v,\\hspace{0.222em}2(u-3v),\\hspace{0.222em}v+u-3v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u-3v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-6v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨2u-3v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-6v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-2v⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(2u-3v)\\cdot (-1)+(2u-6v)\\cdot (3)+(u-2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u+3v+6u-18v+u-2v \\\\  & =5u-17v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z-y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y-z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5u-17v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\color{blue}(5u-17v)}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424097 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-4,1)$ and $B(0,-2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(0,-2)-f(-4,1) \\\\  & =({0}^{2}-2(-2)^{3})-((-4)^{2}-2(1)^{3}) \\\\  & =16-16+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424226 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424229 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2x))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2x)+\\frac{x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(\\pi ,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F})(\\pi ,1) & =2(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\pi )+\\frac{\\pi }{1} \\\\  & =2(1)(1)+\\pi  \\\\  & =2+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424230 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{2}) \\\\  & =2(x+y)+2(y+z)+2(x+z) \\\\  & =4(x+y+z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}(-2,1,-1) & =4((-2)+(1)+(-1)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424328 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-\\sqrt{3},3).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/49/4903ca2ba8cde3f7874e10998a0741035ec7b2dea69a7804eaca64cb552fc572.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}}{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{3}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }r{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424329 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(1,-1)$ and $(1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/43/4371a691b81b2ed1cb5d3468f68cd24f41e8a6b87d378a3206238d58e61791f0.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x+y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424330 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424515 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6f6a5cf9b37fe16f17fe74face946d85b426badf8db710ca4a7ea308a1ff2ec.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=14xy$ and $Q(x,y)=6{x}^{2}y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=14y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(14y+6{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}(14y+6{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(7{y}^{2}+6{x}^{2}y)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(7{x}^{6}+6{x}^{5})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{7}+{x}^{6})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424516 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2ecabe22395006f0c7ef0a3f914087fa324f4d59c7d7886c18907f824744643e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}3x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424517 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b25d12781632bcc03576a79e7fbf1b22806fc68e3f521ecd602f1b0732d8d90d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{7}-x$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2xy-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-1}^{0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[0-((-1)^{2}y-(-1))]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-y-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-(1))-(-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424519 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/86/861b873005c03ea6c25255f2febc975ff153d939780ec46e4f4503cbb50640dc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6xy+y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-{y}^{2}.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=6y+2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6y+2xy)+(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y(2+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424520 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/72/728036ffc90e2476017b3102b6279f9c5900f0e7fc58536e76c9aa038eb04f02.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y+1\\le x\\le {e}^{y},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y+1}^{{e}^{y}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{-y+1}^{{e}^{y}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}+y-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =-{[{e}^{y}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-[(e+\\frac{1}{2}-1)-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424522 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/63/636bfd79297e6fef4b0e56af9b0cb22f2e9a8061e833ea1dcf07e7958b870cdc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{4}+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{3}+5)y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4{x}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & ={x}^{3}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}+5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5({x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=5({x}^{3}+1).$"] [17592186424523 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb0033d2b275442ee92cc0b805c71102641c670ea1b0edba97d31a9262795c83.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=8{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\hspace{0.167em}x\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-1}^{0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\cdot 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =[2{y}^{4}+2{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{4}+2(1)^{2})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424524 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f1b9472021d3973167a7601785803837627476a4e8b1460e42f87e91ecb99d7e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2x+3{y}^{2}.$"] [17592186425876 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7-2{t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+10)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}6t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-6{t}^{2})^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =6t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,\\sqrt{3}].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}6t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+1$, $\\text{d}u=2t\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2t\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2(8-1) \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186427901 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/8d/8dc4ea0ad2bb776fb31132e1ff8d97efdd2c166dc23ffbc8641f8d7034b8d487.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\pi /2}}}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186427902 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9c/9c4ea0ba92e19a201e3944defa6deb0bc1ee76971adaa809521722b6ac31241f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=r,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,r\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3}}}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r}}}^{3}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186427905 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/83/831de8cf1d9833f8674a7e4cb69d01098970eefb9cf576c0537fa7be471c8c2c.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,-2\\le z\\le \\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}2(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\pi }}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta }}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186427906 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b6/b66b8db18b2f5773addc2d00c02f2f15f4a7ead53323418e63bd50457f74466b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -8r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-5\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}+1}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+1}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\pi /2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186430769 "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2}2r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{2}]_{\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}({2}^{2}-{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(4-{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(4-{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & =[4\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}\\theta ]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))-(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =(2\\pi +0)-(\\pi +1) \\\\  & =\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186430947 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0-4+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x-z+2\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{x}^{2}}}}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}x-z+2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3{z}^{2}-4}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186430948 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2z)+6z \\\\  & =4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}4z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(2-(-1))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [2{z}^{2}]_{z=-1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (8-2) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186430949 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+4z) \\\\  & =0+1+4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}5\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =5{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186430950 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}3}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}3}{e}^{z}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot [{e}^{z}]_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =({1}^{3}-{0}^{3})\\cdot 2\\cdot ({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186430951 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+z) \\\\  & =-2+3+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $3$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}2\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{3} \\\\  & =72\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186430952 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y) \\\\  & =2x+xz+0 \\\\  & =2x+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(2x+xz)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2x+xz}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186431049 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t-2\\operatorname{cos}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\operatorname{sin}t+2\\operatorname{sin}2t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186431051 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}2t\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}t{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186431052 "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t)=5{t}^{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-8{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)=-\\mathbf{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}x\\,dy-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\cdot (-8{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{8}\\,\\text{d}t+0 \\\\  & =8[\\frac{{t}^{9}}{9}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186431053 "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨5{\\operatorname{sin}}^{4}t\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-5{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}t\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}t)\\cdot (-5{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{5{\\operatorname{sin}}^{6}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}t}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186431349 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{4}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3}).$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,dx+({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}-(-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot ((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3{r}^{5}}{5}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}(0))\\cdot (\\frac{3(4)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3(1)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =(1-1)\\cdot (\\frac{3(4)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3(1)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186431350 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/ca/cad0f92fdd888243562619b410a200ca040ca43f83bfd15fb124c8a8ecae4df6.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the inner curve ${C}_{2}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{2}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =2-9+8 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186431351 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/7b/7b68ab924946d0509b1666b5c0795a7f8987c32aef88ca3ab72746663e9428a8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =4+9+(-5) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186431353 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot ({3}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =160\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186431357 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/f7/f7869b25194e311c08df6c5b647fe9c53f7064c94eaa5d60fa1fa2551a6b58a1.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =7+(-3)+2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186433780 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $y=\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/89/897028515a29fdc4174d2a101439a81a30895db41ac3d29f311d53e0e6a79e29.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186433781 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(1,\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/10/1060c071df1dbd197c14da529819a34bd4457868fdd4cbe78a7f93f12dbf67da.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta }^{2}4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta }^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta }^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (2-\\operatorname{sec}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(8\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -4)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[8\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -4\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =[(8\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\frac{4\\pi }{3})-0] \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186436855 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨8,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-5,7⟩ \\\\  & =8\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+(-4)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =16+10-28 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) &=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186436857 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 7+4\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =7-8+6 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) &=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186436859 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-5,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-5,4⟩ \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186436860 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-(-1\\cdot 0-(-3)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-6,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨5,-8,-7⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,-6,2⟩ \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)+(-8)\\cdot (-6)+(-7)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186436861 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\hspace{0.167em}-1,\\hspace{0.167em}4⟩\\cdot ⟨-2\\sqrt{2},\\hspace{0.167em}3,\\hspace{0.167em}-5⟩ \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2})(-2\\sqrt{2})+(-1)(3)+(4)(-5) \\\\  & =-8-3-20 \\\\  & =-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186436933 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+2yz \\\\  & =3yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186436934 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y{e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-z{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186436974 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/f0/f0a5627965f71f6eff869a45b1b575b892a67ebba9b1ec969f5dc38ba84cb250.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $3$ centered at $(0,-3).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +6r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +9 & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/75/7515fffda1580baa332df454e7fe53a60f70944215f13d83e0e2269a0101dba6.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta =2\\pi $ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186437273 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=(1-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)\\operatorname{cos}x={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x+(-2\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x+2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =3{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186441615 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,4\\le r\\le 8,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3{r}^{4}}{4}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{4}^{8} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}(0))\\cdot (\\frac{3(8)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(4)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (\\frac{3(8)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(4)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186442249 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t+2\\operatorname{sin}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}t-2\\operatorname{cos}2t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186442250 "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\operatorname{cos}t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t)\\cdot (-3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{3{\\operatorname{sin}}^{4}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186442251 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-4\\operatorname{cos}4t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}4t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}2t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186442252 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{cos}t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186446572 "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\ne \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n![](images/80/80c67e624f960147b7980ee90700be800a414ab1e2a2e47faf7e8821ee0c0623.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0}$ everywhere in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$, we say that $\\mathbf{F}$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\ne \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\ne \\mathbf{0},$ the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186446573 "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\ne \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n![](images/b1/b1520791fcb3808604758b7d8f99443020d12075d4cc8bff78659880575b6ef2.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0}$ everywhere in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$, we say that $\\mathbf{F}$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- Statements III and IV are false. Since $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0},$ the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is irrotational. And, since $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0},$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186446574 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x+2y) \\\\  & =-2x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-2,1)} & =-2(-2)-2 \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186459208 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/48/4822bfa926feccb0a12c14580ac325f4f10a0e4f815d0247b2231bf08b8c2326.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}(1+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+3xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}(1+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+({x}^{2}+3xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3y-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186459263 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}(y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+(\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+10)+2x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/93/93a1c20377ddd0731c6190712d9d6d77551124fa33001334e84f52049bb6b800.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1+2{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(1+2{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 9,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(1-2\\cdot {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186481405 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e570374b37c116e32c3a7762b8cf09a28bdbb3e06b5e67e25837902a89bcd264.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y+\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}x\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186483360 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=0$ everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf{F},$ we say that $\\mathbf{F}$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the length of the vectors' $x$-components increase as we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction. Therefore, the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}\\ne 0,$ the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186483377 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)) \\\\  & =2xyz+{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,-1,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}(-1,-1,2) & =2(-1)(-1)(2)+(-1)^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{-1}{-1}\\right) \\\\  & =4-1+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186483395 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=0$ everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf{F},$ we say that $\\mathbf{F}$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components decrease as we move vertically towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}\\ne 0,$ the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186490232 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,5),B(1,2)$ and $C(2,-4).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,5)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (5) & =-5(0)+6 \\\\ 5 & =6\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does *not* lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(1,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (2) & =-5(1)+6 \\\\ 2 & =1\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (-4) & =-5(2)+6 \\\\ -4 & =-4\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line."] [17592186490279 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,6)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/d6/d6cb6d026c181d79bda5c809729eb070c554c2025a287577df0330d1dd8a03e7.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y+3x=6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6)+3(0) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0)+3(2) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+3x=6$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(6)+(0) & =2 \\\\ 2+0 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(0)+(2) & =2 \\\\ 0+2 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-3x+6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6) & =-3(0)+6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0) & =-3(2)+6 \\\\ 0 & =-6+6 \\\\ 0 & =0\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-3x+6$ is  an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nAll the given equations represented the line in the graph. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""] [17592186490287 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,2)$ and $(4,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/2b/2b9174d86d123228ae24fffeda4d2e4741e4a776f6671f7eff38340a59ba4d40.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-1(0)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =-2+2 \\\\ 0 & =0\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =2+2 \\\\ 0 & =4\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186490291 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-1,-2)$, $B(0,1)$ and $C(1,6).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-1,-2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (-2) & =4(-1)+2 \\\\ -2 & =-2\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,1)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (1) & =4(0)+2 \\\\ 1 & =2\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,6)$ results in true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (6) & =4(1)+2 \\\\ 6 & =6\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line."] [17592186498590 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/b2/b20b2b911c2a417c26fa951d9b28efa296c265df25e9d06b635ef4846267b93b.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =16+6+4 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186511457 "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)=\\mathbf{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}2t]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0]-[-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186511542 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+12\\operatorname{cos}4t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}4t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}2t(-2\\operatorname{sin}2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =6({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
